steps.body:
Every object has a **storage duration**, the rule for when its memory is made and when it goes away. C++ has four:

| Duration | Declared as | Made | Destroyed |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| automatic | a local | where it's declared | at its block's closing brace |
| static | a global, or `static` | before `main`, or a static local on first use | after `main` returns |
| thread | `thread_local` | on its thread's first use | when that thread ends |
| dynamic | `new` | at the `new` | at the `delete` |

To watch it happen, write a `struct`, a type of your own that bundles named members, here one `std::string` called `name`. Give it a **constructor**, a function named after the type that runs when an object is made, and a **destructor**, `~` and the name, which runs when one is destroyed. *Structs and Classes* and *Constructors, Destructors, and RAII*, in the Object-Oriented Programming section, teach all three properly. Here they're a probe that prints:

```cpp
struct Noisy {
    std::string name;
    Noisy(const std::string& n) : name(n) {  // : name(n) starts name as a copy of n
        std::cout << "make " << name << '\n';
    }
    ~Noisy() {
        std::cout << "drop " << name << '\n';
    }
};
Noisy probe("probe");  // make probe, then drop probe as main ends
```

Statics are destroyed after `main` returns, newest first. A static local that's never reached is never made. And an object made with `new` and never deleted is never destroyed at all: its destructor doesn't run, even when the program ends.

`thread_local` gives each thread its own copy. *Threads, Data Races, and Mutexes*, in the advanced track, covers threads. Here a second thread runs `work`, and `join` waits for it to finish:

```cpp harness
struct Noisy {
    std::string name;
    Noisy(const std::string& n) : name(n) {
        std::cout << "make " << name << '\n';
    }
    ~Noisy() {
        std::cout << "drop " << name << '\n';
    }
};

{{code}}
```

```cpp
void work(const std::string& who) {
    thread_local Noisy buffer("buffer");  // one per thread
    std::cout << who << " uses its buffer\n";
}

std::thread worker(work, "worker");  // runs work on a new thread
worker.join();
work("main");
```

The worker's buffer is dropped when the worker ends, before `join` returns. The main thread's buffer is made on its own first use and dropped as the program exits.

