steps.body:
Before `main` runs, the static objects are set in two phases. First, every one is filled with zeros, or with a value the compiler works out itself, like `const int kTick = 5;`. Then each one whose value needs code to run, such as `int g_tickSize = loadTickSize();`, runs that code. Within one file the second phase goes top to bottom. Across files the order is unspecified, and in practice it follows the link order.

So a global that reads another file's global at startup can find it still at zero. Two files on the Mac:

```cpp norun
// config.cpp
int loadTickSize() {
    return 5;  // imagine this reads a config file
}

int g_tickSize = loadTickSize();  // set when the program starts
```

```cpp norun
// main.cpp
#include <iostream>

extern int g_tickSize;  // defined in config.cpp

int g_seenAtStart = g_tickSize;  // also set when the program starts

int main() {
    std::cout << "seen at start: " << g_seenAtStart << ", now: " << g_tickSize << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```text
$ clang++ -std=c++17 config.cpp main.cpp -o first && ./first
seen at start: 5, now: 5
$ clang++ -std=c++17 main.cpp config.cpp -o second && ./second
seen at start: 0, now: 5
```

Same code, a different answer, decided by the order of two file names. That's the **static initialization order fiasco**, and it hides well. With `-O2`, clang works `loadTickSize()` out at compile time and both orders print 5, so the bug can vanish in one build and come back in another.

The usual fix holds the value in a static local, which is set the first time its function runs, whoever calls it first:

```cpp
int& tickSize() {
    static int size = 5;  // set on the first call
    return size;
}
std::cout << tickSize() << '\n';  // 5
```

Since C++11, that first call is safe even when two threads make it at once. When the value is a constant, `constexpr int kTickSize = 5;` avoids the problem entirely, because the compiler sets it. C++20 adds `constinit`, which demands that, as *What C++20 and C++23 Add* shows. Destruction has the mirror problem: a static's destructor that uses another file's static may run after that one is gone.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int loadTickSize() {
    std::cout << "loading tick size\n";  // imagine it reads a config file
    return 5;
}

int readTickSize();  // declared here, defined below

// Globals in one file are set top to bottom, before main starts.
int g_seenAtStart = readTickSize();  // runs first: g_tickSize is still 0
int g_tickSize = loadTickSize();     // runs second

int readTickSize() {
    return g_tickSize;
}

// The fix: a static local is set the first time the function runs.
int& tickSize() {
    static int size = loadTickSize();
    return size;
}

int g_fixedAtStart = tickSize();  // calls it first, so it's ready

int main() {
    std::cout << "main starts\n";
    std::cout << "seen at start: " << g_seenAtStart << '\n';
    std::cout << "now: " << g_tickSize << '\n';
    std::cout << "fixed: " << g_fixedAtStart << ' ' << tickSize() << '\n';
    return 0;
}

