The stack is small and fixed in size: 8 MB for the main thread here and on the Mac (`ulimit -s` prints 8176 there, in KB), and 1 MB by default on Windows. Every other thread gets a smaller one of its own: 2 MB in the sandbox, 512 KB on the Mac. Each call adds a frame and each `return` removes one, so recursion that goes too deep runs off the end of the stack. That's a **stack overflow**, and the program dies:

```cpp error
#include <iostream>

long depth(long n) {
    char frame[1024];  // 1 KB of stack per call
    frame[0] = 1;
    return depth(n + 1) + frame[0];  // never stops
}

int main() {
    std::cout << depth(0) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

The sandbox prints `Segmentation fault`, and the exit status is 139: 128 plus 11, the signal SIGSEGV. The Mac's shell reports the same 139. The cure is small frames and bounded depth: big buffers go in a `std::vector`, and recursion that could go deep becomes a loop.

The heap has no small fixed limit: it grows on demand, as far as the machine's memory allows. `new` calls `operator new`, which calls `malloc`, which hands out pieces of memory it already got from the kernel. When it runs short, glibc's `malloc` on Linux asks for more with one of two **system calls**, requests to the kernel:

- `brk` moves the end of the heap up, for small requests.
- `mmap` maps a fresh block of pages anywhere in the address space. A request of 128 KB or more that doesn't fit in the heap gets one of its own, and `delete` hands it straight back with `munmap`.

Either way the kernel gets involved, which costs far more than reusing memory `malloc` already has. Even 1 byte comes back as a usable 24 here, since `malloc` rounds up and keeps a small header beside each block. On Linux, `strace ./a.out` lists every `brk` and `mmap` a program makes, and macOS's `malloc` gets its memory from the kernel in a similar way. The advanced lesson *Allocations: reserve, Small Buffers, and Hot Paths* counts every call to `operator new` and keeps them out of code that must be fast.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <malloc.h>  // glibc's mallinfo: Linux only

// What malloc has taken from the OS so far (glibc's own counters).
long heapBytes() {
    return mallinfo().arena;  // the heap, grown with brk
}
long mappedBlocks() {
    return mallinfo().hblks;  // blocks that each got their own mmap
}

char* allocate(long n) {
    long heapBefore = heapBytes();
    long mappedBefore = mappedBlocks();
    char* block = new char[n];
    if (mappedBlocks() > mappedBefore) {
        std::cout << n << " bytes: a mapping of its own (mmap)\n";
    } else if (heapBytes() > heapBefore) {
        std::cout << n << " bytes: the heap grew (brk)\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << n << " bytes: from the heap malloc already had\n";
    }
    return block;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << "heap in use before main: " << (heapBytes() > 0 ? "yes" : "no") << '\n';
    char* small = allocate(1'000);
    char* medium = allocate(100'000);
    char* big = allocate(1'000'000);
    delete[] big;  // an mmap block goes straight back to the OS (munmap)
    std::cout << "mapped blocks after delete[]: " << mappedBlocks() << '\n';
    delete[] medium;
    delete[] small;
    return 0;
}

